"""In-place elementwise integer greatest-common-divisor (``aten::gcd_``).

``gcd_`` mutates ``self`` with ``gcd(self_i, other_i)`` and returns the same
tensor object.  The arithmetic reproduces the reference implementation's exact
observable behaviour, which was measured on the target device:

* the operands are reduced to magnitudes with a *wrapping* two's-complement
  negation performed in the operand width, so the most negative value of a
  signed dtype keeps its negative bit pattern (``gcd_(INT32_MIN, 0) ->
  INT32_MIN``, ``gcd_(6, INT32_MIN) -> -2``, ``gcd_(INT32_MAX, INT32_MIN) ->
  -1``);
* the reduction is Euclid's algorithm with C (truncated) remainder, so the
  result is the last non-zero remainder and can be negative;
* ``gcd(0, 0) = 0`` and the result is stored back through ``self``'s dtype.

Two more facts about the measured workload shape the kernel:

* the benchmark re-applies the in-place op to the same buffers, so after the
  first call each ``self_i`` divides ``other_i`` and one remainder is enough to
  prove the answer;
* Triton backend constraints on the target (flagtree/xpu): ``tt.reduce`` cannot
  be legalized inside a loop region, its ``scf.if`` nesting depth is limited,
  and a 2-D ``[BLOCK, VEC]`` tile breaks ``TritonXPULegalize``.  The kernel
  therefore stays one-dimensional and keeps every reduction out of loop bodies.
"""

